how Graham re-establishing a clear lead.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race pairs appointed incumbent Darline Graham against Democrat Annie Andrews in a state with an eight-point Republican lean. Despite that baseline, recent surveys from late September show Graham ahead by only one to three points, and headlines describe the contest as neck and neck. Ratings have begun drifting toward Democrats, with Cook and others softening their Republican ratings, though most still call the race Likely or Safe R.\n\nAndrews has dramatically out-raised Graham, $10.8 million to about $347,000, though Republican outside groups have spent $2.6 million on Graham's behalf. Prediction markets remain skeptical of an upset, pricing Andrews around 14 to 18 percent. The race is closer than South Carolina usually is, but Graham retains the edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2115, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2143, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1474, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2135, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2708, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2623, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2878, "margin": -3.5}]}